 title: Re-estimate size when scope grows; split before review, not after
 when: design
 tags: [size, scope, process]
-check: Was the issue's `size:` re-estimated from the real design (or from a prototype's diff) each time its scope, design or specs grew, and if it is over the raft cap of about 1,500 changed lines, or its specs have tripled since the first estimate, did a split go to the user before the first review round?
+check: Was the issue's `size:` re-estimated from the real design (or from a prototype's diff) each time its scope, design or specs grew, and if it is over the raft cap of about 3,000 changed lines, or its specs have tripled since the first estimate, did a split go to the user before the first review round?
 ---
-`size:` is z-raft's input: it sums the sizes against its cap of about 1,500 lines. An estimate written in round 1 and never touched again lies once the design grows, and an issue that has outgrown the cap is cheaper to split before review than after it.
+`size:` is z-raft's input: it sums the sizes against its cap of about 3,000 lines. An estimate written in round 1 and never touched again lies once the design grows, and an issue that has outgrown the cap is cheaper to split before review than after it.
 
 **Why:** c0265 (z-nile) carried `size: 640` from round 1 to round 18 while its Design grew from one storage choice to six interlocking mechanisms (lease, verdicts, automatic beats, ownership memory, board verdicts, discarded worktrees) and its specs tripled to about 2,300 lines. Every fix in one mechanism opened an edge in another, and 18 rounds (3.5 M tokens) did not converge. The user split it into five issues in one raft, delivered a layer at a time (2026-11-08).
 
diff --git a/z-nile/skills/z-raft/SKILL.md b/z-nile/skills/z-raft/SKILL.md
index c56ffc9de..141dd12b0 100644
--- a/z-nile/skills/z-raft/SKILL.md
+++ b/z-nile/skills/z-raft/SKILL.md
@@ -15,7 +15,7 @@ Work in a rafting worktree: `cd "$(./zn worktree raft-<slug>)"` (any short slug,
   - one type per raft and one project per raft; a raft that is not prepared in the backlog (new, being prepared, active or done) is never pulled in, and a `single: true` raft never takes another issue;
   - defects fold into one open prepared defect raft (the lowest id that still fits), and chores fold the same way;
   - features group only when they share a surface: a Touched path of one overlaps a Touched path of another (or of an open feature raft's member), taken as connected groups; an issue with no Touched modules shares nothing;
-  - a raft that would pass about 1,500 changed lines (the sum of its members' `size:`) starts another; an issue estimated over that is a raft by itself and is never split;
+  - a raft that would pass about 3,000 changed lines (the sum of its members' `size:`) starts another; an issue estimated over that is a raft by itself and is never split;
   - an open raft with a member that has no `size:` takes nothing, because the cap can't be kept: the plan prints `skip <R-id>: <member> has no size:, …` and opens another raft instead (with an argument you may still `fold` into it);
   - a `solo: true` issue is always a raft by itself (it records a user decision, such as sequenced rafts), and nothing is ever folded into a raft that holds one;
   - an issue with no `size:` is skipped and named: send it back to z-design.
